<sn><md>#### Move Local Files to a Remote

For example:

```bash
rclone move -P ~/Documents gd:Backup
```

This moves the contents of:

```text
~/Documents/
```

into:

```text
gd:Backup/
```

After each file is successfully transferred, the original source file is removed.

If you want to preserve the `Documents` folder name:

```bash
rclone move -P ~/Documents gd:Backup/Documents
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Move Remote Files to Local Storage

```bash
rclone move -P gd:Backup ~/Downloads/Backup
```

After successful transfer, the files are removed from:

```text
gd:Backup
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Move Between Two Remotes

For example:

```bash
rclone move -P gd:Backup onedrive:Backup
```

Conceptually:

```text
Google Drive
      │
      │ Transfer
      ▼
OneDrive
      │
      ▼
Source files removed
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Move a Single File

Use `moveto` to move one file to an exact destination:

```bash
rclone moveto -P ~/Documents/notes.pdf gd:Backup/notes.pdf
```

You can also rename the file while moving:

```bash
rclone moveto -P ~/Documents/notes.pdf gd:Backup/my-notes.pdf
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Move Specific Files

Use `--include`:

```bash
rclone move -P ~/Documents gd:Backup \
  --include "*.pdf"
```

This moves only PDF files. Successfully transferred PDF files are removed from the local source.

For specific files:

```bash
rclone move -P ~/Documents gd:Backup \
  --include "notes.pdf" \
  --include "assignment.pdf"
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Move an Entire Directory

```bash
rclone move -P ~/Documents/Projects gd:Backup/Projects
```

Rclone moves the contents of `Projects` into the destination folder.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Preview Before Moving

Since `move` removes source files, use `--dry-run` first:

```bash
rclone move --dry-run ~/Documents gd:Backup
```

Then, if everything looks correct:

```bash
rclone move -P ~/Documents gd:Backup
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### `copy` vs `move`

| Feature                                        | `rclone copy` | `rclone move` |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------- |
| Transfers files                                | ✅             | ✅             |
| Source files remain                            | ✅             | ❌             |
| Source files removed after successful transfer | ❌             | ✅             |
| Extra destination files deleted                | ❌             | ❌             |
| Suitable for backup                            | ✅             | ⚠️            |
| Suitable for transferring files                | ⚠️            | ✅             |
| Supports remote → remote                       | ✅             | ✅             |
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Important Safety Note

`rclone move` does **not normally delete the source file until it has been successfully transferred**. However, because it ultimately removes your original files, you should use:

```bash
rclone move --dry-run SOURCE DESTINATION
```

before moving important data.

A safe workflow is:

```bash
# Step 1: Preview
rclone move --dry-run ~/Documents gd:Backup

# Step 2: Perform the move
rclone move -P ~/Documents gd:Backup
```

This lets you verify the source and destination paths before transferring and removing your original files.
